    {
      "id": "library/10665-83270/blocks/prose-page-002",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 2",
      "pageStart": 2,
      "pageEnd": 2,
      "target": "../sections/page-002.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "A52/DIV/10\n\n3. La nécessité de se concentrer sur les éléments fondamentaux d’une bonne santé publique et d’assurer\nl’équité d’accès à ceux-ci a été reconnue dès le départ. Toutefois, la constatation qui a par la suite dominé les\ndébats a été la suivante : l’établissement des priorités est un processus complexe et essentiellement politique. Si\ndes approches plus systématiques, plus rationnelles et plus transparentes sont nécessaires, les ministres ont à faire\nface à des demandes concurrentes de la part de différents groupes de la population.\n\n4. Le débat a reposé notamment sur l'hypothèse selon",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarizes the round table on priority-setting at the 1999 World Health Assembly, which found that setting health priorities is a complex and largely political process in which ministers face competing demands. Health ministers are not the only actors, since debt, donor conditions and finance ministries constrain spending, and stated priorities need real expenditure plans that are hard to keep in decentralized systems. Emergencies, conflict, HIV/AIDS, short-term needs such as failing hospitals, the East Asian financial crisis and steep budget falls in the former Soviet Union further complicate priority-setting.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-83270/blocks/prose-page-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 3",
      "pageStart": 3,
      "pageEnd": 3,
      "target": "../sections/page-003.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "A52/DIV/10\n\ngratuitement. La fixation des priorités exige que chacun reconnaisse qu’il a la responsabilité de participer aux\nécisions difficiles concernant l’utilisation des ressources lorsque celles-ci sont comptées.\ndi difficil t l’utilisation d Il Il t ti\n\n11. Dans la plupart des pays, les besoins sanitaires varient. La fixation des priorités au niveau national doit\npermettre une prise de décision au niveau local.\n\n12.  L’instauration d’un consensus autour des priorités nationales est déterminante, mais une direction claire\nest également capitale. Cela fait partie du travail des politiciens",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Continues the 1999 round table on priority-setting, stressing local decision-making, clear leadership, genuine consultation and the strong influence of donors, whose own agendas and earmarked funding can work against national priorities, and suggesting longer-term partnerships and aid within an overall public spending plan. It then opens the round table on hospital investment, stating that governments must ensure universal, equitable and affordable hospital care, with explicit hospital policy and a balanced network suited to each country's needs.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
